A teacher account sees a much shorter drawer than an admin's, and that is deliberate — nearly
everything you need is in five places.

## Your drawer

| Entry | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| **Dashboard** | Where you land |
| **My Class → Students** | Your class roster — and where you mark attendance |
| **My Class → Attendance** | Your class's attendance history: daily, weekly, term |
| **My Timetable** | Your own week, read-only |
| **My Attendance** | Your own attendance record |
| **My Profile** | Your details, photo and password |

Depending on what your institute has granted you, you may also see **Exams** (for entering results),
**Progress Reports**, **Students**, and **Send Messages**.

**My Class** only appears if you are set as a class teacher. If you teach subjects across several
classes but own none of them, you won't have it — that is correct, not a fault.

## The order to learn them in

1. **[Marking attendance](/docs/educonnect-teacher-taking-attendance)** — the one thing you will do
   every single morning.
2. **[Reading your class's attendance](/docs/educonnect-teacher-class-attendance)** — day-wise,
   week-wise and term views, and which to use when.
3. **[My Timetable](/docs/educonnect-teacher-my-timetable)** — including how substitutions show up.
4. **[Entering exam results](/docs/educonnect-tutorial-enter-exam-results)** when exams come round.
5. **[Progress reports](/docs/educonnect-teacher-progress-reports)** at the end of term.

## If a screen is missing

Access is granted per screen, not per role, so two teachers at the same school can legitimately see
different menus. If something this guide describes isn't in your drawer, ask whoever administers
your institute — it is a permission, and it can be switched on.

